a simple dry fruit that develops from a simple carpel and usually dehisces (opens along a seam) on two sides."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-legumes"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legumes"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":5,"groups":[["Kazakh cuisine","cuisine/kazakh-cuisine",[["Chorba","dish/chorba"]]],["Kyrgyz cuisine","cuisine/kyrgyz-cuisine",[["Chorba","dish/chorba"]]],["Tajik cuisine","cuisine/tajik-cuisine",[["Chorba","dish/chorba"]]],["Turkmen cuisine","cuisine/turkmen-cuisine",[["Chorba","dish/chorba"]]],["Uzbek cuisine","cuisine/uzbek-cuisine",[["Chorba","dish/chorba"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beans","ingredient/beans"],["Vegetables","ingredient/vegetables"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/e/e7/Various_legumes.jpg/500px-Various_legumes.jpg","w":500,"h":748,"by":"Keith Weller, Agricultural Research Service, USDA","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Various_legumes.jpg"}},"ingredient/rice-vermicelli":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rice-vermicelli","n":"Rice vermicelli","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 7 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Rice vermicelli is a thin form of rice noodle. It is sometimes referred to as \"rice noodles\" or \"rice sticks\", but should not be confused with cellophane noodles, a different Asian type of vermicelli made from mung bean starch or rice starch rather than rice grains themselves."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rice-vermicelli"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice_vermicelli"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Jalangkote","dish/jalangkote"],["Ketoprak (dish)","dish/ketoprak-dish"],["Laksa","dish/laksa"],["Sop saudara","dish/sop-saudara"]]],["Burmese cuisine","cuisine/burmese-cuisine",[["Mohinga",